content.text:
The diagnostic log line is garbled/hacky: `temp={_temperature?.Invoke(...) == 0} t={0}` — invalid because `_temperature?.Invoke(..., out var t)` can't declare out var in a conditional access argument? Actually you can't use `out var` inside a null-conditional invocation easily; `t` scope issues. Let me write clean diagnostics:

```csharp
if (!_diagnosed)
{
    _diagnosed = true;
    var utilResult = _utilization(_device, out var utilization);
    var tempResult = _temperature is null ? -1 : _temperature(_device, 0, out var temp);
    Log.Info($"nvml util ret={utilResult} gpu={utilization.Gpu}; temp ret={tempResult} temp={temp}");
}
```
But temp declared inside if with out var remains in scope. Fine.

Rewrite sample block. Note I already call `_utilization` before; restructure:

```csharp
if (_utilization is not null)
{
    var result = _utilization(_device, out var utilization);
    if (!_diagnosed)
    {
        _diagnosed = true;
        var tempResult = _temperature is null ? -1 : _temperature(_device, 0, out var temp);
        Log.Info($"nvml util ret={result} gpu={utilization.Gpu} mem={utilization.Memory} tempRet={tempResult} temp={temp}");
    }
    if (result == 0) snapshot.GpuPercent = utilization.Gpu;
}
```
`temp` out var in conditional expression—`_temperature is null ? -1 : _temperature(_device, 0, out var temp)` then using `temp` afterwards: definite assignment? `temp` may be unassigned when null branch → compile error. Use:
```csharp
uint temp = 0;
var tempResult = _temperature is null ? -1 : _temperature(_device, 0, out temp);
```

Fix.

