161MB RAM and 9.8s CPU over ~5 minutes (mostly startup + screenshot?). Hmm, 9.8s CPU is high. The overlay polls every 300ms, enumerating windows (EnumWindows ~hundreds of windows + GetClassName) per overlay → that could add up: ~3.3 polls/sec × window enumeration. 9.8s over 5 min = 3% of one core. Too high for a tray monitor.

Optimizations (ponytail, real):
1. Cache the taskbar handle; only call GetWindowRect each tick (cheap) and re-enumerate only if the cached handle is invalid/moved? Handle stays valid. Cache per overlay after first resolution; verify with `Native.GetWindowRect(handle)` and `IsWindow(handle)`.
2. Reduce polling to 500ms.
3. Metrics tick every 1s: LHM CPU update each tick is expensive (MSR reads ~ tens of ms). That's likely the CPU usage: LHM `_cpu.Update()` per second. Actually if unelevated, LHM failed to init → no LHM updates. So CPU is from EnumWindows.

Also the settings window's 2s status timer + preview updates are minor.

Implement TaskbarLocator cache:
```csharp
public static TaskbarInfo? DescribeSingle(IntPtr hWnd) ...
```
Overlay:
```csharp
private IntPtr _taskbarHwnd;
private TaskbarInfo? ResolveTaskbar()
{
    if (_taskbarHwnd != IntPtr.Zero)
    {
        var info = TaskbarLocator.Describe(_taskbarHwnd);
        if (info != null) return info;
        _taskbarHwnd = IntPtr.Zero;
    }
    var all = TaskbarLocator.GetAll();
    ... pick monitor ...
    _taskbarHwnd = picked?.Handle ?? IntPtr.Zero;
    return picked;
}
```
Make `Describe` public static in TaskbarLocator. If the taskbar handle changes (explorer restart), re-enumerate.

Cache is also invalid if the user moves the taskbar to another monitor: `Describe` returns the monitor device of the handle; if it no longer matches `_monitorDevice`, re-enumerate.

Implement:
```csharp
private TaskbarInfo? FindTaskbar()
{
    if (_taskbarHwnd != IntPtr.Zero)
    {
        var info = TaskbarLocator.Describe(_taskbarHwnd);
        if (info is not null && string.Equals(info.MonitorDevice, _monitorDevice, StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase))
            return info;
    }
    var info2 = TaskbarLocator.GetAll()
        .Where(t => string.Equals(t.MonitorDevice, _monitorDevice, ...))
        .OrderByDescending(t => t.IsPrimary).FirstOrDefault();
    _taskbarHwnd = info2?.Handle ?? IntPtr.Zero;
    return info2;
}
```
Note primary taskbar handle is stable. Secondary handles may be recreated on monitor changes; fallback handles.

Then polling at 400ms (taskbar moves are animated? Windows doesn't animate taskbar moves much). 500ms is fine. With cached handle, each tick is one GetWindowRect + MonitorFromWindow + GetMonitorInfo; cheap.

Also compute the tray rect each tick (FindWindowEx + GetWindowRect) — cheap. Or only when resizing. Fine.

Metrics: reading every second is okay.

Another CPU source: settings window open with preview → App updates preview every tick. Minor.

RAM 161MB: WPF app with NVML + single-file self-contained. LHM assemblies loaded? Unelevated init failed but LHM managed assemblies + driver extraction may retain memory (~20MB). Acceptable. Could lazily not init LHM when not elevated (skip init if not admin) to save memory; but the app can't elevate itself later. On this machine, when elevated, LHM works. For memory, 161MB is a bit high but fine for a 16GB system. Not worth optimizing now.

CPU time measurement may include the initial build? No, per process.

Let's implement the caching and 500ms timer.

Also, we could stop polling entirely when the taskbar rect hasn't changed by using Win32 events, but caching is enough.

Implement:
- Make `TaskbarLocator.Describe` public (rename to `DescribeHandle`).
- Overlay caches.

Then rebuild, restart, measure CPU after some minutes? We can measure CPU over 30s with Get-Process CPU delta.

Let's do the code fixes (supports percent, blocks UI, taskbar cache), then rebuild and republish.

One more consideration: `OverlayWindow.Position()` calls `UpdateLayout()` every 300-500ms — this forces a layout pass each tick, which is wasteful. Only needed after content changes. Remove `UpdateLayout()` from Position; instead call Position after `Reload` (which already triggers layout) and rely on SizeChanged. But when taskbar rect changes DPI, ActualWidth stays; we just reposition. We need ActualWidth/Height current — they are after the last layout. The initial call in Loaded after layout is fine. So remove `UpdateLayout()` from Position to cut work. If a window size changes, SizeChanged fires and calls Position, where ActualWidth is updated. Good.

Also `Native.GetDpiForWindow` per tick — cheap, cache when DPI changes? Keep.

Implement.

