The user says: "شايف ال vram مش فى نفس مستوى ال ram اللى فوقيها" — "You see the VRAM is not on the same level as the RAM above it."

Looking at the zoom: Row 1: RAM 6.2/15.8G. Row 2: VRAM 0.0/4.0G. The vertical alignment issue: the "VRAM" block is shifted slightly right relative to "RAM"? Let's look at the final zoom image:

Row 1: `↑ 0.0 Mbps  [CPU icon] CPU 9%   [RAM icon] RAM 6.2/15.8G`
Row 2: `↓ 0.0 Mbps  [GPU icon] GPU 0%  [VRAM icon] VRAM 0.0/4.0G`

The labels "RAM" and "VRAM" start at different x positions because the preceding blocks have different widths: "CPU 9%" vs "GPU 0%" — same-ish; but the icons are different widths? Also CPU has no temp now so width of CPU block = icon + "CPU" + "9%" vs GPU block = icon + "GPU" + "0%" — nearly equal but VRAM label starts slightly right of RAM.

Looking at the image: "RAM" starts at x≈840 (in the zoom), "VRAM" starts at x≈700? Hmm, in the final zoom, row1 text: "RAM 6.2/15.8G" at ~840..1090; row2 "VRAM 0.0/4.0G" at ~700..1090? Actually looking at the last image: row 1 "RAM 6.2/15.8G" starts at ~835; row 2 "VRAM 0.0/4.0G" starts at ~700? Let me re-inspect the final.png mentally: 

The zoom final.png width 1410 (3x of 470px starting at 1285). Text positions:
- Row1: arrows ~320, "0.0 Mbps" ~370, CPU icon ~550, "CPU 9%" ~590, RAM icon ~790, "RAM" ~840, "6.2/15.8G" ~905.
- Row2: arrows ~320, "0.0 Mbps" ~370, GPU icon ~575?, "GPU 0%" ~620, VRAM icon ~690, "VRAM" ~730, "0.0/4.0G" ~810.

So indeed VRAM starts left of RAM because the "GPU 0%" block is narrower than "CPU 9%"? No, they look similar... Actually the GPU/VRAM column is not aligned with the CPU/RAM column. The user notices the columns are not aligned vertically: RAM is above VRAM but they're at different horizontal positions.

The root cause: each row is an independent StackPanel with variable-width blocks; values change width (CPU 9% vs 100%), so columns drift. To align the two rows in columns, we need a Grid with shared column widths: each column contains the row1 and row2 blocks for that metric pair.

Design change: pair blocks by position index? Row1 col2 = CPU (with temp), Row2 col2 = GPU. Hmm, but the reference image aligns columns: net (up/down), CPU/GPU, RAM/VRAM. So rows are pairs, and the pairs should be aligned.

Implementation options:
1. Layout both rows in a Grid with columns sized to the max width of the corresponding row1/row2 cells. Blocks are ordered; pair index i in row1 with index i in row2. Create a Grid with N columns (N = max(row1 count, row2 count)), each column containing a StackPanel with the row1 block (if any) and row2 block (if any)? That would stack them vertically in a single cell → two rows: Grid with 2 rows and N columns. Each cell holds a MetricView; ColumnDefinition Width=Auto sizes to the widest cell. Perfect alignment.

However, with this design, the spacing between block pairs is controlled by column gaps; and if the user has 4 blocks in row1 and 2 in row2, columns 3-4 have only row1 blocks — fine.

So replace _row1/_row2 StackPanels with a Grid: 2 rows, N columns. Each cell contains the MetricView with Margin right = spacing (except last). This aligns columns automatically.

But an important nuance: blocks have independent widths; in the reference, the network column has arrows where the up/down are left-aligned, spanning the same column. Aligning columns would align "0.0 Mbps" values and put the CPU next at the same x as GPU. That looks cleaner.

Alignment of values within a column: ColumnDefinition Auto width = max(row1 block width, row2 block width). Since "CPU 9%" and "GPU 0%" differ slightly, the column width = max; the narrower block will be left-aligned within the cell, leaving a gap before the next column. The *next* column starts at the same x for both rows → labels RAM/VRAM align. Good.

But within the CPU/GPU column, the GPU percent value won't right-align with the CPU percent value. In the reference, values are right-aligned per column ("88%" and "4%" both right-aligned). Not critical; the user's complaint is the VRAM not being on the same level as RAM (vertical alignment of columns).

Alternative simplest fix: make each MetricView fixed-width per kind so columns align naturally: set a MinWidth/Width per block kind (Net 92, CPU/GPU 100, RAM/VRAM 95). But values differ (RAM 6.2/15.8G vs VRAM 0.0/4.0G) and the icons/labels differ ("RAM" vs "VRAM") → fixed widths could still misalign if not identical.

Grid approach is the correct small change. Let me implement:

```csharp
private readonly Grid _grid = new();
```
In constructor:
```csharp
_grid.ColumnDefinitions.Clear(); ...
```
Rebuild:
```csharp
_grid.Children.Clear();
_grid.ColumnDefinitions.Clear();
_grid.RowDefinitions.Clear();
_grid.RowDefinitions.Add(new RowDefinition { Height = GridLength.Auto });
_grid.RowDefinitions.Add(new RowDefinition { Height = GridLength.Auto });

var row1 = _settings.Blocks.Where(b => b.Enabled && b.Row == 1).OrderBy(b => b.Order).ToList();
var row2 = ... Row != 1 ...
var columns = Math.Max(row1.Count, row2.Count);
for (int i = 0; i < columns; i++) _grid.ColumnDefinitions.Add(new ColumnDefinition { Width = GridLength.Auto });

for each i: if i < row1.Count add view at (row 0, col i); if i < row2.Count add view at (row 1, col i).
```
Then trailing spacing: for each column except the last, keep the right margin; the last column's items should have margin 0. But if a column has only one cell, the other cell empty. The total width = sum of max widths per column.

If spacing is a Margin.Right on each MetricView, then for the last column having both row1 and row2, both margins are trailing; set the last column's views margin right 0.

But there's a subtlety: if a row is shorter, its MetricsViews have no trailing elements; fine.

Another subtlety: the original row2 stacked under row1; now with the Grid, row heights are Auto and the total height is the same. Row2 visibility: if row2.Count==0, don't add the row or set `_grid.RowDefinitions[1].Height = new GridLength(0)`; simplest: only add the second RowDefinition if row2.Any(). Rebuilding a Grid each time is fine.

This also fixes the alignment of the CPU/GPU column.

Wait, but the user might mean something else: "الـ VRAM مش في نفس مستوى الـ RAM اللي فوقيها" = VRAM is not at the same level/line as RAM above it. Could mean vertical alignment: VRAM's baseline is lower/higher than RAM? In the screenshot, both rows look evenly spaced. Or it could mean the VRAM *label* is not aligned under RAM (horizontal). I think they mean horizontal alignment ("مستوى" = level/line).

Looking at the screenshot: Row1: "RAM 6.2/15.8G", Row2: "VRAM 0.0/4.0G". Indeed the VRAM block starts further left than RAM because "GPU 0%" is narrower than "CPU 9%"? Actually in the zoom, "GPU 0%" ends earlier, so VRAM icon starts earlier. Yes, the columns don't line up.

With the Grid, "RAM" and "VRAM" will start at the same x. 

Implement in MeterView. Also MetricView margin: currently `Margin = new Thickness(0,0,settings.Spacing,0)`. For the Grid, each cell's view has that margin. For the last column, set margin right 0. But if the max column is only in row1 and row2 is shorter, margins are fine.

One more alignment improvement: within a column, right-align the values? Not requested. Skip.

Also the row spacing: vertical StackPanel rows had 0 spacing; the Grid rows with Auto height produce the same. MetricView has VerticalAlignment Center.

Edge: a row can have duplicate order values; sorted.

